	</item>
	<item>
		<title>Care and keeping</title>
		<link>/care/</link>
		<pubDate>2026-08-30 12:00:00</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[admin]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">/?p=102</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p class="prose__lead">None of this is difficult, but a few habits are the difference between a piece lasting years and lasting one summer.</p>
  <h2 class="prose__sub">The three rules</h2>
  <p>Rinse and dry anything that has been in the sea or a pool. Salt and chlorine are what actually strip plating, and Miami is full of both.</p>
  <p>Put jewelry on after perfume, lotion and hairspray, never before. Alcohol dulls plating and clouds crystal faster than anything else.</p>
  <p>Store pieces flat and separately. Long chains tangle into each other in a way that takes an hour and a pin to undo.</p>
  <h2 class="prose__sub">Cleaning</h2>
  <p>A dry soft cloth is enough for almost everything. For crystal, breathe on it and buff it the way you would a pair of glasses.</p>
  <p>Never use jewelry dip or silver polish on plated brass. It strips the plating and the plating does not come back.</p>
  <h2 class="prose__sub">Sweat</h2>
  <p>Sweat is not the enemy, salt left sitting on metal overnight is. Wipe a piece down before bed after a heavy night and it will outlast anything you have owned.</p>]]></content:encoded>
		<excerpt:encoded><![CDATA[]]></excerpt:encoded>
		<wp:post_id>102</wp:post_id>
		<wp:post_date><![CDATA[2026-08-30 12:00:00]]></wp:post_date>
