	<item>
		<title>What you are paying for when you buy crystal</title>
		<link>/crystal-is-labour/</link>
		<pubDate>2026-08-30 12:00:00</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[admin]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">/?p=109</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p class="prose__lead">La Reina costs more than everything else combined. Here is exactly where that money goes.</p>
  <h2 class="prose__sub">It is hours, not materials</h2>
  <p>The crystal in a chain bralette is not the expensive part. The expensive part is that somebody sits with it for the better part of two days, setting stones into a shaped mesh one at a time, checking the line every few rows because a single stone set half a degree off will throw the whole run out by the time it reaches the edge.</p>
  <p>Machine set crystal exists and it is much cheaper. It also sits flat and dead, because the whole point of a prong is that it lifts the stone off the backing and lets light get underneath it.</p>
  <h2 class="prose__sub">Why the fringe is cut unevenly</h2>
  <p>On Diabla the fringe is cut to staggered lengths rather than a straight line. A straight fringe hangs like a curtain and moves as one solid block. Staggered lengths break it up so each strand swings on its own timing, which is why it keeps moving for a half second after you have stopped.</p>
  <p>That is a deliberate choice that costs more to make and it is the difference between the piece looking alive and looking like a costume.</p>]]></content:encoded>
		<excerpt:encoded><![CDATA[]]></excerpt:encoded>
		<wp:post_id>109</wp:post_id>
		<wp:post_date><![CDATA[2026-08-30 12:00:00]]></wp:post_date>
		<wp:post_date_gmt><![CDATA[2026-08-30 12:00:00]]></wp:post_date_gmt>
		<wp:comment_status><![CDATA[closed]]></wp:comment_status>
		<wp:ping_status><![CDATA[closed]]></wp:ping_status>
		<wp:post_name><![CDATA[crystal-is-labour]]></wp:post_name>
		<wp:status><![CDATA[publish]]></wp:status>
		<wp:post_parent>0</wp:post_parent>
		<wp:menu_order>0</wp:menu_order>
		<wp:post_type><![CDATA[post]]></wp:post_type>
		<wp:post_password><![CDATA[]]></wp:post_password>
		<wp:is_sticky>0</wp:is_sticky>
	</item>
	<item>
		<title>Body chain goes under clothes, not instead of them</title>
		<link>/under-not-instead/</link>
		<pubDate>2026-08-30 12:00:00</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[admin]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">/?p=110</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p class="prose__lead">The most common misread of the category, and the reason most people who try it never go back.</p>
  <h2 class="prose__sub">The photographs lie a little</h2>
  <p>Almost every body chain photograph online shows it worn on bare skin against a plain background, because that is the clearest way to show what the object is. It is a terrible guide to how anybody actually wears one.</p>
  <p>A waist chain under a white shirt that shows through when you move is doing more work than the same chain worn bare. A chest drape over a plain black slip changes the whole outfit. The chain is a layer, not the outfit.</p>
  <h2 class="prose__sub">Where to start</h2>
  <p>Buy a waist chain first, wear it under everything for two weeks, and see how often you think about it. That is genuinely how most people end up buying the rest. It is $30, it goes on in the morning, and nobody knows it is there except you until you decide otherwise.</p>]]></content:encoded>
		<excerpt:encoded><![CDATA[]]></excerpt:encoded>
		<wp:post_id>110</wp:post_id>
		<wp:post_date><![CDATA[2026-08-30 12:00:00]]></wp:post_date>
		<wp:post_date_gmt><![CDATA[2026-08-30 12:00:00]]></wp:post_date_gmt>
		<wp:comment_status><![CDATA[closed]]></wp:comment_status>
		<wp:ping_status><![CDATA[closed]]></wp:ping_status>
		<wp:post_name><![CDATA[under-not-instead]]></wp:post_name>
		<wp:status><![CDATA[publish]]></wp:status>
		<wp:post_parent>0</wp:post_parent>
		<wp:menu_order>0</wp:menu_order>
		<wp:post_type><![CDATA[post]]></wp:post_type>
		<wp:post_password><![CDATA[]]></wp:post_password>
		<wp:is_sticky>0</wp:is_sticky>
	</item>
	<item>
		<title>Why your waist chain slides, and how we stopped it</title>
		<link>/why-it-slides/</link>
		<pubDate>2026-08-30 12:00:00</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[admin]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">/?p=111</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p class="prose__lead">The single most common reason people give up on body chain, and it is a solvable engineering problem.</p>
  <h2 class="prose__sub">The problem is the taper</h2>
  <p>A waist is not a cylinder. It narrows going up and flares going down, so a plain loop of chain sitting on it is on a slope. Gravity does the rest. Every step you take, the chain finds a slightly wider spot and settles into it, and forty minutes later it is sitting on your hip bones instead of your waist.</p>
  <p>Sizing down does not fix this. It just means the chain digs in while it slides.</p>
  <h2 class="prose__sub">What actually works</h2>
  <p>Two things. Weight distribution and friction. Our waist chains carry their clasp and extender at the back so the heaviest point sits in the hollow of the spine, which is the one place on the torso that is naturally concave. The chain wants to stay there.</p>
  <p>The thigh chain needed more, because a thigh has a much steeper taper than a waist. It took eleven versions before we put a thin silicone lined band behind the front chain, hidden completely, and the sliding stopped. That band is the whole reason that piece exists.</p>]]></content:encoded>
		<excerpt:encoded><![CDATA[]]></excerpt:encoded>
		<wp:post_id>111</wp:post_id>
